1
00:00:01,190 --> 00:00:05,610
Earlier we talked at length about
identifying and managing risks.

2
00:00:05,610 --> 00:00:09,240
Risk management is absolutely
critical to a project success.

3
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:13,900
So in this video, we'll introduce
additional techniques for managing risks.

4
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:17,990
Let's start with a reminder of
the definition of risk management:

5
00:00:17,990 --> 00:00:21,556
Risk management is the process of
identifying potential risks and

6
00:00:21,556 --> 00:00:24,675
issues which could impact a project,
then evaluating and

7
00:00:24,675 --> 00:00:29,450
applying steps to address the effects
of the identified risks and issues.

8
00:00:29,450 --> 00:00:30,755
One way to manage risks, and

9
00:00:30,755 --> 00:00:34,315
hopefully, prevent any risks from
materializing is to focus on managing

10
00:00:34,315 --> 00:00:37,889
the changes and dependencies, as well
as any scope creep in your project.

11
00:00:39,290 --> 00:00:42,177
If you can manage those two things,
both changes and

12
00:00:42,177 --> 00:00:47,510
dependencies and scope creep, other types
of risks become much easier to manage.

13
00:00:47,510 --> 00:00:49,417
If your dependencies are met on time,

14
00:00:49,417 --> 00:00:52,250
your team is less likely
to fall behind schedule.

15
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:56,063
If your scope is tightly-managed, you're
less likely to incur changes to your

16
00:00:56,063 --> 00:00:58,280
budget or
be forced to extend your timeline.

17
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:02,758
Brainstorming with your team is one
of the most effective techniques for

18
00:01:02,758 --> 00:01:05,030
identifying risks in a project.

19
00:01:05,030 --> 00:01:09,094
Your teammates likely bring skills and
experience from previous projects,

20
00:01:09,094 --> 00:01:13,970
which can help suss out similarities and
keep you from repeating any issues.

21
00:01:13,970 --> 00:01:18,120
As we brainstorm with our team,
it's best to create a risk register.

22
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:20,784
As a refresher,
a risk register is a table or

23
00:01:20,784 --> 00:01:24,270
chart that contains your
team's list of risks.

24
00:01:24,270 --> 00:01:26,647
You want to pose questions
to your team, like

25
00:01:26,647 --> 00:01:31,730
what could improve the outcome of the
project, or what could hurt or hinder it?

26
00:01:31,730 --> 00:01:34,340
You'll list them all as if/then statements.

27
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:40,850
For example, if a given event happens,
then here's how the project is impacted.

28
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:43,806
To help prioritize risks
within your risk register,

29
00:01:43,806 --> 00:01:45,980
you can calculate your risk exposure.

30
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:49,567
Risk exposure is a way
to measure the potential

31
00:01:49,567 --> 00:01:53,600
future loss resulting from
a specific activity or event.

32
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:58,600
A good method to calculate risk exposure
is to build a matrix like this one.

33
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:03,358
When building out your matrix,
you'll use two variables: risk impact and

34
00:02:03,358 --> 00:02:04,750
probability.

35
00:02:04,751 --> 00:02:08,307
Write "risk impact" at the top,
horizontal axis, and

36
00:02:08,307 --> 00:02:12,590
write "probability" on the side,
vertical axis.

37
00:02:12,590 --> 00:02:17,583
Mark high, medium, and low along each
axis as well, across the top from left

38
00:02:17,583 --> 00:02:20,594
to right and
down the side from top to bottom,

39
00:02:20,594 --> 00:02:24,573
because that's how you're
going to chart risk exposure.

40
00:02:24,573 --> 00:02:29,171
Add each risk to the chart at the cross
section of the impact the risk might have

41
00:02:29,171 --> 00:02:34,320
on your project and probability or
likelihood of the risk coming to pass.

42
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:38,208
This is one technique, but
whatever strategy you use to examine

43
00:02:38,208 --> 00:02:41,952
your risk exposure,
your risks will need to be prioritized so

44
00:02:41,952 --> 00:02:46,490
that you know and your team knows which
ones to give immediate attention to.

45
00:02:46,490 --> 00:02:50,764
For anything that has a high impact
on your project, even if it has a low

46
00:02:50,764 --> 00:02:55,270
probability of occurring, make sure
to have a mitigation plan in place.

47
00:02:56,720 --> 00:03:00,590
How will you handle this risk
if it actually materializes?

48
00:03:00,590 --> 00:03:02,652
While a risk register is a great tool,

49
00:03:02,652 --> 00:03:05,819
it's still likely that some
unforeseen risk will arise.

50
00:03:06,990 --> 00:03:08,850
It's almost impossible to account for

51
00:03:08,850 --> 00:03:11,740
every single risk over
the course of a project.

52
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:14,824
That's where the ROAM technique can help.

53
00:03:14,824 --> 00:03:19,500
The ROAM technique—which stands for
resolved, owned, accepted, and

54
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:23,619
mitigated—is used to help manage
actions after risks arise.

55
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:28,930
Once a risk has materialized,
you need to decide what to do with it.

56
00:03:28,930 --> 00:03:32,034
If a risk has been eliminated and
will not be a problem,

57
00:03:32,034 --> 00:03:34,620
it goes into your "resolved" category.

58
00:03:34,620 --> 00:03:38,666
If you give a team member ownership over
a certain risk and entrust them to handle

59
00:03:38,666 --> 00:03:43,640
it, that risk goes into the "owned" category and is monitored through to completion.

60
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:48,310
If the risk has been "accepted," it has been agreed that nothing will be done about it.

61
00:03:48,310 --> 00:03:52,669
Finally, if some action has been taken
such that the risk has been mitigated,

62
00:03:52,669 --> 00:03:55,445
either reducing the likelihood
of it occurring or

63
00:03:55,445 --> 00:04:00,420
reducing the impact to the project,
it goes into the "mitigated" category.

64
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:04,842
After each risk is placed into a category,
the team will discuss each risk and

65
00:04:04,842 --> 00:04:07,950
decide which should be prioritized.

66
00:04:07,950 --> 00:04:08,980
Awesome.

67
00:04:08,980 --> 00:04:12,089
Now you've learned a bit more about
how to differentiate risks and

68
00:04:12,089 --> 00:04:16,228
issues, as well as some new
techniques to manage various risks.

69
00:04:16,228 --> 00:04:19,724
Next, we'll learn about how to share
these risks with your stakeholders and

70
00:04:19,724 --> 00:04:21,748
a technique called escalation.

71
00:04:21,748 --> 00:04:23,198
Sound intriguing?

72
00:04:23,198 --> 00:04:24,828
Head to the next video to learn more.
